<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingayatism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Virashaivism, Lingayat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lingayatism, also called Virashaivism, is a Shaiva devotional tradition founded in 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, a minister at the Kalachuri court of Kalyani. It rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> hierarchy, temple ritual and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priesthood, and its followers wear a small linga as a sign of personal devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. Its vachana poems are a major part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature, and Lingayats are a large community in Karnataka today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lingayatism.jpg" alt="Lingayatism" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of Kannada and Culture, Government of Karnataka · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basavanna.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
